## 2. Exact Degree Lattice

The class (\delta) is primitive and the second circle is topologically
trivial. The integral Leray-Serre/Gysin sequence and Kunneth theorem give

\[
H^2(X,\mathbb Z)\simeq H^2(S,\mathbb Z)/\mathbb Z\delta.
\]

Because (H\cdot\delta=0), the integer (H\cdot D) is well defined for a
line-bundle Chern class represented by (D\in H^2(S,\mathbb Z)). For

\[
\omega_t=\sqrt t\,\pi^*\omega_H
  +t^{-1/2}i\zeta\wedge\bar\zeta,
\]

the only nonzero contribution to
(\int_Xc_1(L)\wedge\omega_t^2) is proportional to
(H\cdot D). The proportionality factor is positive and independent of
(t). We therefore normalize

\[
\deg_t H=H^2=2,
\qquad \mu_t(F)=\frac23.
\]

The argument below is consequently uniform for every (t>0).

## 3. No Degree-One Effective Divisor

Brinzanescu-Halanay-Trautmann prove that every holomorphic vector bundle on a
nontrivial principal elliptic bundle has fiber degree zero. If an irreducible
effective divisor on (X) dominated (S), its associated line bundle would
have positive degree on a generic elliptic fiber. Hence every effective
divisor on (X) is vertical and comes from an effective divisor on (S).

It remains to exclude an effective K3 curve (C) with (H\cdot C=1). The
degree-two map (S\to\mathbb P^2) sends such a curve birationally to a line,
and the covering involution supplies a second component over the same line.
Thus such a curve exists exactly when the branch sextic restricts to a square
of a binary cubic on some line.

The exact verifier restricts the explicit branch sextic to each of the three
affine charts of the dual projective plane and equates its seven coefficients
to those of

\[
(c_0u^3+c_1u^2v+c_2uv^2+c_3v^3)^2.
\]

Over (\mathbb F_5), all three seven-equation ideals have reduced Groebner
basis `[1]`. This is emptiness over the algebraic closure, not a search over
only five-rational lines. The incidence of a projective line and a projective
binary cubic square root is projective over (\mathbb Z). A nonempty
characteristic-zero fiber would therefore specialize to a point over
(\overline{\mathbb F}_5), contradicting the three unit ideals. Hence the
explicit q79 K3 has no splitting line and every nonzero effective divisor on
(X) has normalized degree at least two.

## 4. Independence of the Extension Classes

Write the two components of the extension class as

\[
e_1,e_2\in\operatorname{Ext}^1(I_\Gamma(H),\mathcal O_X).
\]

The Hartshorne-Serre local-to-global map sends them to the selected sections
(T_1,T_2\in H^0(\omega_\Gamma(-H))). These sections are nonzero and carry
the distinct nontrivial clock characters (\omega) and (\omega^2). If
(a e_1+b e_2=0), functoriality gives (aT_1+bT_2=0). Distinct character
spaces are linearly independent, so (a=b=0). Thus (e_1,e_2) are
independent in the full Ext group, not only in a character ledger.

Applying `Hom(-,O_X)` to the extension now gives

\[
\operatorname{Hom}(F,\mathcal O_X)
=\ker\left(\mathbb C^2\xrightarrow{(e_1,e_2)}
\operatorname{Ext}^1(I_\Gamma(H),\mathcal O_X)\right)=0.
\]

## 5. Rank-One Subsheaves

It is enough to test saturated subsheaves. For a saturated rank-one
subsheaf (A\subset F), replace (A) by its reflexive hull; the map extends
across codimension two and the slope is unchanged, so (A) may be treated as
a line bundle.

If (A\to I_\Gamma(H)) is zero, then (A\to\mathcal O_X^{\oplus2}) is
nonzero. Therefore (A^{-1}) has a section and (\deg A\le0).

Otherwise (I_\Gamma(H)\otimes A^{-1}) has a nonzero section. If
(\deg A>0), integrality leaves the following possibilities for
(H\otimes A^{-1}):

- degree one, excluded by the no-splitting-line theorem;
- degree zero, whose nonzero section is nowhere vanishing and cannot vanish
  along the nonempty codimension-two curve (\Gamma);
- negative degree, which has no section.

Thus every rank-one subsheaf has slope at most zero, strictly below
(\mu(F)=2/3).

## 6. Rank-Two Subsheaves

Let (G\subset F) be saturated of rank two. Its quotient is rank-one
torsion-free, so

\[
F/G=I_Z\otimes L,
\qquad (F/G)^{\vee\vee}=L,
\]

for a codimension-at-least-two subscheme (Z). Composing with
(I_Z\otimes L\hookrightarrow L) gives a nonzero map (F\to L), and the
codimension-two defect does not change degree.

If (\deg L<0), both (H^0(L)) and (H^0(L(-H))) vanish. If
(\deg L=0) and (L\not\simeq\mathcal O_X), the same holds; if
(L\simeq\mathcal O_X), the Ext-independence result gives
(\operatorname{Hom}(F,L)=0). If (\deg L=1), the exact Hom sequence forces
a nonzero section of (L), producing the excluded degree-one effective
divisor. Therefore every actual quotient has (\deg L\ge2), and

\[
\mu(G)=\frac{2-\deg L}{2}\le0<\frac23=\mu(F).
\]

This treats saturation, reflexive hulls and codimension-two ideal sheaves
explicitly. Hence (F) is stable. Duality preserves slope stability, so
(\kappa_{\rm hol}=F^\vee) is stable as well.

## 7. Hermite-Einstein Consequence

The balanced ray is Gauduchon. The Li-Yau Kobayashi-Hitchin theorem, or its
twisted extension when used on gerbe charts, supplies a Hermite-Einstein Chern
connection for each stable bundle. For (\kappa_{\rm hol}),

\[
i\Lambda_{\omega_t}F_h=\lambda_t I_3,
\qquad \lambda_t\propto\mu_t(\kappa_{\rm hol})=-\frac23.
\]

Subtracting one third of the trace gives
(\Lambda_{\omega_t}F_h^0=0). This is the projective HYM connection. The
nonzero determinant remains present and must be canceled or transported by
the already selected B-field/projective construction before a physical
determinant-one hidden endpoint can be claimed.

